weave is the per-repo EXECUTION engine: a local, filesystem-based
orchestrator that runs agentic CLIs (codex, claude, agy, opencode, ...)
in parallel over ONE repo. Seed a queue of issues (runs), fan tools out
across them in isolated git-clone workspaces so they never clobber each
other, then pull the converged work back into the repo. Entirely local —
no server, no forge.

For the CROSS-REPO plan/handoff layer above weave — the sprint kanban,
conductor lease, continuity record, and checkpoints — see `bashy sprint`.

Common-case usage:

  bashy weave add "fix null deref in cache" --priority p0
  bashy weave start -- codex --dangerously-skip-permissions "<body>"
  bashy weave list                           # runs in flight
  bashy weave pull                           # absorb merged work

Usage:
  weave [flags]
  weave [command]

Available Commands:
  abandon      Tear down a weave (workspace + branch + any running wrapper)
  add          Seed an issue into the queue
  attach       Interactively watch and steer a running subagent
  autopilot    Autonomously drive THIS repo's run queue (claim→launch→verify→merge) with failover
  baton        Show THIS repo's single-driver lock + campaign handoff note (cross-repo handoff: `bashy sprint`)
  check        List every subcommand and its implementation status
  comment      Append a comment to an issue's history thread
  comments     Show an issue's history thread
  doctor       Reap limbo states and audit the lifecycle of every open item
  finalize     Terminalize an explicitly observed-idle interactive run
  fleet        Show each fleet member's availability (installed? on PATH? cooling down? signed in? model usable?)
  gc           Report (or, with --apply, reclaim) orphan and legacy weave state across every queue on this machine
  guide        Print the CONDUCTOR playbook (a.k.a. `weave docs`) — how to take over + run a campaign
  heartbeat    Periodically drive autopilot in the background with idle shutdown
  help         Help about any command
  kill         Stop the running wrapper precisely, preserve workspace + branch
  link         Record that one issue needs another first (parallel safety, as data)
  list         Show active weaves (--all: every queue on the machine)
  log          Print (or --follow) the captured PTY log of an issue's subagent
  memory       Inspect or export persistent weave memory
  next         Peek at the next issue 'weave start' would claim (non-mutating)
  open         Surface an issue's workspace path (file:// URL)
  pause        Cleanly suspend every running weave worker
  point        Set an issue's story points (sprint planning; 8 = ~30m cap)
  prio         Set an issue's priority tier (or --auto to LLM-rank the queue)
  prune        Remove workspace directories and merged branches for terminal items
  pull         Fast-forward your local main with the merged agent branches
  recall       Recall persistent memory observations related to a query
  reconcile    Settle a retained reservation after verifying its recorded child group stopped
  remember     Append a manual persistent memory observation
  reset        Tear down every weave and remove all weave state for this project
  resume       Relaunch paused weave workers from their recorded launch specs
  reverify     Refresh an issue's git and verify attestation from its workspace
  review       Re-verify submitted work in a fresh clean-room checkout
  salvage      Review and merge the committed work of a killed/failed item (via pull's gate)
  say          Inject a line into a running subagent's terminal
  sealed-image Build Linux agent images for sealed booths
  shell        Drop into a shell inside the issue's workspace
  split        Break an oversized issue into children, keeping the link to the parent
  start        Allocate a workspace and launch an agent
  status       Show an issue's reconciled state, merge status, and verify result
  wait         Block until issue(s) reach a terminal state

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for weave

Use "weave [command] --help" for more information about a command.
